  "worked_solution": "First, we take the factor $(-4)$ outside the summation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}-4\u0394{x}_{i}=-4{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}\u0394{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}\u0394{x}_{i}$ is just the length of the interval $[2,10],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}\u0394{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2})+\u22ef+({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{0})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{x}_{3}}-\\cancel{{x}_{2}})+\u22ef+({x}_{m}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}+{x}_{m} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}-{x}_{0} \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-4{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}\u0394{x}_{i}=(-4)\\cdot 8=-32.\n$$",
